# 24-01-26 – Journey – Ubuntu DevOps Node Setup (MacBook)

## Summary
Today I repurposed an old MacBook into a dedicated Ubuntu-based DevOps node. The focus of the session was not application development, but infrastructure setup, secure access, and environment consistency across my lab.

This machine will act as a portable DevOps control node alongside my existing WSL2 and Windows environments.

## What I Worked On
- Installed Ubuntu Desktop on repurposed MacBook hardware
- Configured networking over Wi-Fi
- Enabled and verified SSH access
- Installed Tailscale and joined the device to my existing tailnet
- Verified private network access to internal services:
  - Pi-hole admin dashboard
  - Jellyfin media server
- Installed Google Chrome to support browser-based labs and documentation
- Generated GitHub SSH keys on the Ubuntu node
- Successfully authenticated to GitHub over SSH
- Cloned my `wutang-dev` repository onto the machine
- Created and pushed Project 05 documenting the setup

## What I Learned
- Context (current working directory) matters a lot when working with Git
- SSH key files should never be executed directly — they are handled by the SSH agent
- Tailscale provides a clean way to access internal services without exposing ports
- Treating setup as a first-class project keeps my learning organised and reviewable
- Splitting infrastructure setup from service deployment (Nginx later) makes projects clearer

## What Challenged Me
- Momentary confusion when Git commands failed due to being in the wrong directory
- Understanding why SSH keys return “permission denied” when run directly
- Slowing down instead of forcing commands helped avoid mistakes

## Outcome
By the end of the session, the Ubuntu MacBook was fully integrated into my lab:
- Secure access working
- Services reachable over the private network
- GitHub workflow consistent with my other environments

This feels like a solid foundation for future DevOps, container, and cloud projects.
